From: Grische <opensource@grische.xyz>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 10:07:01 +0200
Subject: wifi: ath10k: reset the HTT tx queue state when the firmware starts

Since commit 426e10eaf76d ("ath10k: implement push-pull tx"), the
firmware reports its tx mode in HTT tx mode switch indications, and the
host keeps the reported mode in htt->tx_q_state. Nothing resets this tx
mode when the firmware stops, so a firmware booted by
ath10k_core_restart() or by a later ath10k_start() inherits the tx mode
of the previous firmware.

In push-pull mode the host sends data frames only when the firmware
asks for them with a fetch indication. If the old firmware was in
push-pull mode, the host stays in push-pull mode when the new firmware
starts. On a QCA9984 the new firmware started in push mode and sent no
fetch indication before its first tx mode switch indication. That
indication arrived 2.2 s to 2.4 s after "device successfully recovered"
in three runs. In one of these runs, pings sent during that time got no
reply.

Since commit 9ec34a86195a ("ath10k: fix Tx DMA alloc failure during
continuous wifi down/up"), the queue state buffer, struct htt_q_state,
is kept across firmware starts as well. The host therefore passes the
new firmware a buffer that still holds the count and map entries of the
peers of the old firmware.

Reset the tx mode fields and zero the queue state buffer at every start
in ath10k_htt_tx_start(), and sync the buffer to the device.

Tested-on: QCA9984 hw1.0 PCI 10.4-3.9.0.2-00157

Fixes: 426e10eaf76d ("ath10k: implement push-pull tx")
Fixes: 9ec34a86195a ("ath10k: fix Tx DMA alloc failure during continuous wifi down/up")
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5 sparse
---
 drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath10k/htt_tx.c | 26 ++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 26 insertions(+)

--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath10k/htt_tx.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath10k/htt_tx.c
@@ -459,6 +459,26 @@ static int ath10k_htt_tx_alloc_txq(struc
 	return 0;
 }
 
+static void ath10k_htt_tx_txq_reset(struct ath10k_htt *htt)
+{
+	struct ath10k *ar = htt->ar;
+	size_t size = sizeof(*htt->tx_q_state.vaddr);
+
+	spin_lock_bh(&htt->tx_lock);
+
+	htt->tx_q_state.enabled = false;
+	htt->tx_q_state.mode = HTT_TX_MODE_SWITCH_PUSH;
+	htt->tx_q_state.num_push_allowed = 0;
+
+	if (htt->tx_q_state.vaddr) {
+		memset(htt->tx_q_state.vaddr, 0, size);
+		dma_sync_single_for_device(ar->dev, htt->tx_q_state.paddr,
+					   size, DMA_TO_DEVICE);
+	}
+
+	spin_unlock_bh(&htt->tx_lock);
+}
+
 static void ath10k_htt_tx_free_txdone_fifo(struct ath10k_htt *htt)
 {
 	WARN_ON(!kfifo_is_empty(&htt->txdone_fifo));
@@ -529,6 +549,12 @@ int ath10k_htt_tx_start(struct ath10k_ht
 	spin_lock_init(&htt->tx_lock);
 	idr_init(&htt->pending_tx);
 
+	/* The queue state buffer and the push-pull mode outlive the
+	 * firmware. Reset them, so a new firmware does not inherit those
+	 * of the old one.
+	 */
+	ath10k_htt_tx_txq_reset(htt);
+
 	if (htt->tx_mem_allocated) {
 		/* The tx memory can come from the start of a firmware that
 		 * did not need the queue state buffer.
